{
  "dom_id": "step-q86352",
  "name": "Question 13",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14212968/assets/8f4638863dbc6bdfbff00d20d2153bb73eb7779719bbc7235d730539cb92329e.png)\n\nAngela observes that a drone is flying horizontally at a constant height of $5\\,\\text{m}$ above the ground. If the drone is flying in the opposite direction from Angela's location at a speed of $1\\,\\text{m/s},$ determine the rate at which the angle of elevation $\\theta $ decreases at the moment when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14212968/assets/80b92831e73b31464fad3adeb1b2e3d9eed0a0df29e8f30b21bddd5f45bde230.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the drone's altitude, and $x$ is the horizontal distance between the drone and Angela. The quantity $y=5\\,\\text{m}$ does not change with time because the altitude of the drone is constant.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{5}{x} \\\\ x & =5\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-5{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-5{csc}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =-5(2)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 1 & =-20\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =-0.05.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the angle of elevation is *decreasing* at a rate of $0.05$ radians per second.",
